# Findings assessed and dismissed, one per line, by gitleaks fingerprint. An
# entry here is a claim that someone looked, so each one carries why.
#
# Nothing may be added to this file to silence a finding that has not been
# assessed. A credential that is real is rotated and the history dealt with;
# it does not get a line here.
#
# That rule is enforced by review, not by the scan: this file is read from the
# checked-out tree, so a line added in the same diff as the secret it dismisses
# turns the job green. It is the one suppression lever secret-scan.yml leaves
# open on purpose, because unlike a `gitleaks:allow` comment it is a diff to a
# root file whose whole content is claims someone has to sign off on.
#
# Two things about the fingerprints themselves, both of which decide how many
# lines one assessed finding costs:
#
# A `commit:file:rule:line` id from the history scan binds the dismissal to
# one commit, so the same string reached by another commit needs its own line
# - and any history rewrite invalidates every entry here at once. The working
# tree scan emits a three-field `file:rule:line` id instead, which carries no
# commit and therefore suppresses that file and line across all of history;
# prefer the four-field form unless the finding genuinely is about the
# checked-out file rather than about a commit.

# A synthetic bearer token inside the test that asserts the log filter
# REDACTS bearer tokens - the assertion is that it does not survive into the
# rendered message. It was never issued: the engine mints its token at
# startup into the data directory, and this value only ever existed as a
# string literal in a test. The current fixture reads
# `EXAMPLE-not-a-real-token-000000000` so neither a scanner nor a reader has
# to make this judgement again; these lines cover the commits that still hold
# the older, realistic-looking one. Those commits keep it, because it grants
# nothing and rewriting history costs every clone and fork a diverged tree -
# a real hazard traded for a cosmetic one.
#
# Two entries for one fixture because the merge that brought it to main
# reintroduces it as its own diff against the first parent, which is what the
# history scan's --diff-merges flag exists to read. A merge commit carrying
# content is exactly the case that flag was added for; that it also surfaces
# an already-assessed fixture a second time is the cost of seeing it at all.
